No, we had to get the
world's longest flying device

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:15.240
that could be thrown from hand to hand.

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:17.530
We had to get the Aerobie.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:18.900
- It's quite an amazing--
- A-E-R-O--

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:20.950
- Flying disk.
- B-I-E.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:22.350
For those of you Googling this.

00:21:22.350 --> 00:21:23.193
The flying ring.

00:21:24.805 --> 00:21:26.110
- I think a boomerang version as well.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.080
They made two different sizes,
maybe three different sizes.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.090
- It actually comes back to you.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:31.690
But they do not float.

00:21:31.690 --> 00:21:33.490
They will fly a long way.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:34.323
- They will sink in water.

00:21:34.323 --> 00:21:35.156
- And they will sink in water

00:21:35.156 --> 00:21:38.710
because we've lost a few
Aerobies in some ponds.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:40.860
- And not just ponds, they fly so far,

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.130
sometimes they would
just go into the woods

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:43.981
and we would never ever find 'em again.

00:21:43.981 --> 00:21:45.330
- Let me tell you,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.550
some of the most exhilarating
experiences I think

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:51.360
we ever had were just getting
so far from each other

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:54.520
that we were questioning
whether we were still

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:55.860
looking at our friend.

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:56.770
- Yeah.

00:21:56.770 --> 00:22:00.365
- And chucking this Aerobie
and then just seeing it soar

00:22:00.365 --> 00:22:04.743
and then the thrill of
seeing that person catch it.

00:22:05.713 --> 00:22:08.050
I gotta hand it to us, man, we were good

00:22:08.050 --> 00:22:08.883
at throwing an Aerobie.

00:22:08.883 --> 00:22:10.670
- We weren't great at
catching them though.

00:22:10.670 --> 00:22:11.870
- Well it's not hard to catch it.

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:13.840
We were good at catching it.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.360
If you don't catch it, it's pointless.

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:17.280
It's like a fail.
- It catches the wind

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:19.426
and sort of hovers and whoa!

00:22:19.426 --> 00:22:21.870
- You'd think you were about to catch it

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:24.380
and you'd be right here with
your hands like splayed out

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.540
in front of your face
and then all of a sudden,

00:22:26.540 --> 00:22:29.730
when it was like five feet
out and rapidly approaching,

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:33.850
it would catch a breeze like
a hawk going over a canyon.

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:34.930
Like catching--
- A thermal.

00:22:34.930 --> 00:22:38.260
- A thermal, and it would
just, it would take off

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:41.708
and it would go another
400 yards it felt like.

00:22:41.708 --> 00:22:42.820
- It felt like.

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:45.630
- It'd go another 38 yards.

00:22:45.630 --> 00:22:46.737
I would take one of those back in time.

00:22:46.737 --> 00:22:49.710
- And there's no place in Los
Angeles to throw one of these.

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:51.780
There's no open space large enough.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.093
- The reason why this is top
of mine is because Lando,

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:00.220
we had some unstructured time
where we were hangin' out

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:02.240
a couple weekends ago and I was like,

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:04.210
whatever you wanna do bud, let's do it.

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:06.910
And his answer immediately was

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:08.360
I wanna go buy a tether ball.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:10.520
That was his answer.
- Yeah.

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.350
This is a child that is used
to not having a lot of space.

00:23:13.350 --> 00:23:14.270
- So he went--
- He needs a ball

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:15.700
tied to a pole.
- Right.

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:16.830
- That's its idea of fun.

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:19.370
- They play tether ball at school

00:23:19.370 --> 00:23:22.400
and he fancied himself a semi-pro.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.010
- Now you didn't buy him one did you?

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:25.410
- Hell yeah I bought him one.
- Okay.

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.670
- I ask him whatever
he wanted, I'll do it,

00:23:27.670 --> 00:23:29.820
so I called the Big 5 and I was like

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:31.480
do you have tether balls
and they were like yeah.

00:23:31.480 --> 00:23:32.313
- Of course they do.

00:23:32.313 --> 00:23:34.410
That's pretty much what they're known for.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.895
- Tether Balls R Us I think is
what they used to be called.

00:23:37.895 --> 00:23:39.100
They were like yeah we got
those so we go over there,

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:40.900
I'm like whoa, they only have one left.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.100
I was like this woman was risking a lot

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:45.548
in just that snap answer.

00:23:45.548 --> 00:23:46.750
- Well it's probably been there for weeks.

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:48.470
- She should have said
we only got one left.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:50.020
Better get over here right now.

00:23:51.000 --> 00:23:54.160
I was like Lando, we're
just, snatch it, buddy.

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:55.850
- You're buying the one tether ball

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:57.850
people have bought in 2019.

00:23:57.850 --> 00:23:59.460
Like okay now call the factory,

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.640
we gotta get another tether ball

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:02.840
because they're comin' in to get it.

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.830
- As we were leaving
with that tether ball,

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:06.320
I saw a guy walk in and I was like,

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:09.293
I looked at Lando, I was like,
he's gonna be disappointed.

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:13.690
I could tell he wanted a tether ball.

00:24:13.690 --> 00:24:17.087
Before we checked out I went
past and they had an Aerobie

00:24:17.087 --> 00:24:18.590
and I was like Lando, look at this

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:20.360
and I told him the story
that I just told you.

00:24:20.360 --> 00:24:21.890
- Way better than a tether ball.

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:24.080
- And we also bought a $10 Aerobie.

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:25.280
- They don't have the big ones though.

00:24:25.280 --> 00:24:26.690
- Yeah they do, yep.

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:27.959
- Okay you got a big one?
- Yeah.

00:24:27.959 --> 00:24:29.090
- Where you gonna throw that?

00:24:29.090 --> 00:24:32.280
- Well we went to the park and
they had a tether ball pole.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.840
That's why he knew he
wanted to buy a tether ball

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:35.920
'cause--
- Oh you didn't buy the pole

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:37.570
and the base, you just bought
the ball and the string.

00:24:37.570 --> 00:24:39.690
- Right.
- Find your own pole.

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:42.560
- A legit pole at the nearby playground.

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:44.240
- Bring your own ball and tether.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:47.380
- There was no ball or tether
there, it was just a pole.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:48.650
- Wow what is the world coming to?

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:50.590
- I know man, you can't leave
a tether ball hanging around.

00:24:50.590 --> 00:24:52.140
Someone's gonna take that.
- Oh yeah.

00:24:52.140 --> 00:24:53.551
Just like rims.
- Taking the rims

00:24:53.551 --> 00:24:56.433
off a souped up Civic.

00:24:56.433 --> 00:25:00.190
- Interesting that you
would take an Aerobie back.

00:25:00.190 --> 00:25:03.260
So you think that would
gain you fame and fortune?

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:04.913
'Cause that was kinda
how I approached this.

00:25:04.913 --> 00:25:05.803
- No.

00:25:07.510 --> 00:25:10.590
Because I think it would be
cool and people would think

00:25:10.590 --> 00:25:14.788
I was awesome but then as
I was making the rounds,

00:25:14.788 --> 00:25:17.730
selling tickets for my exhibitions

00:25:17.730 --> 00:25:19.310
of throwing the Aerobie and whatnot

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:22.680
and I would also educate the people

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:26.660
about the aerodynamic
design which would then lead

00:25:26.660 --> 00:25:28.900
to the invention of the airplane wing.

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:31.160
Because I would go back that far in time.

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:31.993
- Oh.
- 'Cause you know,

00:25:31.993 --> 00:25:34.326
even on the Aerobie
packaging it talks about

00:25:34.326 --> 00:25:37.520
how it achieves lift like a wing

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:41.910
because of the way the lip
is designed, I believe.

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:43.840
So I would actually go back in time,

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:46.120
I was gonna just say I
was gonna take a wing

00:25:46.120 --> 00:25:47.604
of a plane with me.

00:25:47.604 --> 00:25:49.010
- Yeah but then the Wright
brothers would have invented

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:51.900
a giant Frisbee and we
still wouldn't be flying.

00:25:51.900 --> 00:25:54.570
You'd probably screw the whole thing up.

00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:56.423
It's like, no, Orville!

00:25:57.340 --> 00:25:58.470
Trash the plans!

00:25:58.470 --> 00:25:59.580
- I can tell by your--
- There's a man

00:25:59.580 --> 00:26:01.000
with a flying disk.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:03.360
- I can tell by your ridicule
that you are tracking

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:05.680
with my statement.
- So would you take it

00:26:05.680 --> 00:26:07.780
to the Wright brothers?
- But I do disagree with it.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:11.380
I would pre-live the Wright brothers.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.920
I would be there in the late 1800s.

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:17.500
- Why don't you just skip the
Aerobie and go for a plane?

00:26:17.500 --> 00:26:18.800
- I already answered that.
- Okay.

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:20.223
- The wow factor.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.200
Two for one.
- I think the wow factor

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:26.673
of inventing aviation 'cause
who invented the Aerobie

00:26:26.673 --> 00:26:28.527
in this universe.
- That's 'cause the plane

00:26:28.527 --> 00:26:30.000
already existed.
- Do you know his name?

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:30.940
No, you don't.

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:32.640
- Well if the plane didn't exist then,

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:34.980
you would know his name and
that's why I'm going back

00:26:34.980 --> 00:26:37.450
in time with an Aerobie to invent flight!

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:38.823
- I took a different tack.

00:26:39.921 --> 00:26:43.940
I would, this is the
difficult thing to take back,

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:44.810
I admit that.

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:48.367
I would take Public
Storage back. (chuckles)

00:26:48.367 --> 00:26:49.340
- What, oh.

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:50.810
You greedy.

00:26:50.810 --> 00:26:52.450
I see where you're going with this.

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:54.650
- The guy who invented
Public Storage is a guy

00:26:54.650 --> 00:26:56.180
named Brad Hughes.

00:26:56.180 --> 00:26:57.730
He is worth $2.2 billion.

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:58.563
- Good gosh.

00:26:59.753 --> 00:27:00.705
- In 1980--

00:27:00.705 --> 00:27:03.190
- All he did was nothing.

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:05.963
- No, the story goes like this, my friend.

00:27:07.770 --> 00:27:09.810
He needed to store some stuff

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.230
and he had a warehouse
and it was completely full

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:14.150
and he was like, what am I gonna do?

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:16.450
And he came up with the idea for what

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:20.177
he called private storage
which actually makes

00:27:20.177 --> 00:27:22.590
a lot more sense.
- Yeah it does.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.680
- But then when he took it to market,

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:28.180
he had changed it to Public Storage.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:29.420
- For marketing reasons?

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:30.670
- He's like 85 years old.

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:32.505
The dude's worth over two billion dollars

00:27:32.505 --> 00:27:34.073
because he just came up with the idea

00:27:34.073 --> 00:27:37.614
that there should be little
cubby holes for adults

00:27:37.614 --> 00:27:40.883
out in the world that
you can just pay for.

00:27:41.820 --> 00:27:44.490
And no one thought of this until the '80s.

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.747
I mean, take that, Aerobie.

00:27:46.747 --> 00:27:47.610
You know what I'm saying?

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:50.350
You just go back, I would
go back with, in fact,

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:53.840
my time machine would
probably be the storage bin.

00:27:53.840 --> 00:27:56.730
And then I would just
unsheathe it and come out

00:27:56.730 --> 00:27:59.560
and be like, this is
not only a time machine,

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:01.360
that's not really what's important.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:02.760
Put your stuff in here.

00:28:02.760 --> 00:28:03.913
Pay a monthly fee.

00:28:05.030 --> 00:28:05.930
Hand me your money.

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:07.200
Now, here's the other thing.

00:28:07.200 --> 00:28:09.690
'Cause I also was thinking
about the time period.

00:28:09.690 --> 00:28:12.354
You gonna go back to the late 1800s?

00:28:12.354 --> 00:28:13.660
- Okay okay okay.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:15.270
- Before antibiotics?

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:19.280
I'm going back to 1980, brother.

00:28:19.280 --> 00:28:20.185
You know what I'm saying?
- Tom Cruise movies.

00:28:20.185 --> 00:28:25.180
- You basically have all the
luxuries of modern times.

00:28:25.180 --> 00:28:27.840
There's less channels first of all.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:30.570
I take all my storage money

00:28:30.570 --> 00:28:33.350
and I just cruise around in a boat.

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.593
You could come your your
Aerobie and we could toss it,

00:28:35.593 --> 00:28:38.420
I mean, my boat would be
so big that you could throw

00:28:38.420 --> 00:28:40.590
a full-size Aerobie on it.

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:42.050
- But flight wouldn't exist

00:28:42.050 --> 00:28:44.310
'cause I wouldn't have gone back in time.

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:45.170
- Okay.

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:47.620
So they're not mutually exclusive.

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:49.750
You could also--
- I think Public Storage

00:28:49.750 --> 00:28:52.890
is like the one thing that the
majority of people on earth

00:28:52.890 --> 00:28:57.290
when polled could agree
upon as being abhorrent.

00:28:57.290 --> 00:28:58.970
Like there's just something about it.

00:28:58.970 --> 00:29:00.240
- Yeah but here's the thing--
- That's like--

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:01.073
- You don't need to--
- You know what,

00:29:01.073 --> 00:29:02.560
I'm having a negative response

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:04.330
to just you saying Public Storage.

00:29:04.330 --> 00:29:05.650
- Okay how 'bout this?

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:08.410
Beanie Babies, that
guy's worth $1.9 billion.

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:10.044
- Oh is this your runner up?

00:29:10.044 --> 00:29:12.010
- All you have to do is
take a Beanie Baby back

00:29:12.010 --> 00:29:13.658
and you can take that back to 1986

00:29:13.658 --> 00:29:15.543
'cause that's when he invented that.

00:29:15.543 --> 00:29:16.376
- That guy's a chump, man.

00:29:16.376 --> 00:29:17.540
- But it's a lot easier to
travel with a Beanie Baby--

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:18.373
- Stuffed animal--
- Through time.

00:29:18.373 --> 00:29:19.206
- [Link] With beads in it.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:20.953
- You know?

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:23.440
So maybe I just, you know what--

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:26.460
- At least that fits your
brand to talk about beans.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:29.090
- I'd take a storage bin
full of Beanie Babies back.

00:29:29.090 --> 00:29:32.040
And I'd be like I got
two ideas for you fools.

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:34.260
- Be like well I gotta
get a lot of Beanie Babies

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:37.960
before I can justify this
Public Storage thing.

00:29:37.960 --> 00:29:39.370
- I think we've answered that.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:44.370
- Sean-na Brown asks, is it
better to be knowledgeable

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:45.650
or better to be ignorant?

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:48.780
In other words, is ignorance truly bliss

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:50.990
or is it better to have
knowledge of something

00:29:50.990 --> 00:29:54.600
even if it means it can
cause pain or chaos?

00:29:54.600 --> 00:29:56.410
I'd say a corollary question

00:29:57.350 --> 00:29:59.973
which we gleaned from Facebook.

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:05.170
From Megan Elizabeth Taylor, yes.

00:30:05.170 --> 00:30:07.350
That Megan Elizabeth Taylor.

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:12.203
Would you rather be a worried
genius or a joyful simpleton?

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:16.193
Is ignorance truly bliss?

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.470
And how did you approach this question?

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:23.313
In your brain.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:26.170
I don't know your answer of course.

00:30:26.170 --> 00:30:31.170
- Well I took it, would
I rather be the kind of,

00:30:32.210 --> 00:30:35.850
my state of being as a
person would be someone

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:39.150
who is knowledgeable about
everything including the things

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:42.593
that might potentially bring
me anxiety and trouble.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:48.150
Or someone who is just unawares of things

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:49.300
that might bring worry.

00:30:50.349 --> 00:30:55.349
And so as someone who is at
least somewhat knowledgeable

00:30:57.680 --> 00:31:01.920
about enough things to bring
myself anxiety and trouble--

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:02.753
- Uh-huh.

00:31:02.753 --> 00:31:07.560
- I would say that my knee-jerk
reaction is to say that I...

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.985
Me personally in the current state,

00:31:12.985 --> 00:31:14.830
I wanna know things.

00:31:14.830 --> 00:31:16.480
When we talked about the--

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:19.115
- The simulation, I mean,
all of that stuff, yeah.

00:31:19.115 --> 00:31:22.010
You're always opting for knowledge.

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:24.260
- And we talked about the--
- Are you going the other way?

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:27.010
- No no, I'm saying me,

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:31.536
this version of me wants
to know things, including,

00:31:31.536 --> 00:31:34.130
we talked about this on 23andMe.

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:38.140
Maybe we talked about this
but you can basically check,

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.267
yes I want to know if I've got--

00:31:41.850 --> 00:31:44.090
- Predisposition to Parkinson's disease.

00:31:44.090 --> 00:31:45.885
- Right, do I have the
specific genetic markers

00:31:45.885 --> 00:31:47.500
to these illnesses?

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:49.861
- I actually don't know if
that's one specifically.

00:31:49.861 --> 00:31:52.460
- I don't know what they are
but I checked all of those

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:54.120
and looked at all the information.

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:55.670
As soon as I had the opportunity to,

00:31:55.670 --> 00:31:57.470
my instinct was just to look.

00:31:57.470 --> 00:31:59.878
- Right but in the way

00:31:59.878 --> 00:32:00.711
this is framed--
- But that's not how

