00:44:37.550 --> 00:44:39.903
on the way to that meeting, she was like,

00:44:41.140 --> 00:44:42.910
what was the show she asked us if we saw?

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:43.743
It was like...

00:44:47.380 --> 00:44:48.530
Dead?

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:49.880
- Dead to Me?

00:44:49.880 --> 00:44:52.020
- She was like have you seen Dead to Me?

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:53.840
No I never even heard of it.

00:44:53.840 --> 00:44:56.940
She's like well it's a good concept.

00:44:56.940 --> 00:44:59.630
I don't how well it was
executed but it's binge-worthy.

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:00.743
And I'm thinking,

00:45:02.420 --> 00:45:06.480
I'm not in my 20s, I don't
sit around and just look

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:09.540
for stuff that's borderline binge-worthy.

00:45:09.540 --> 00:45:12.520
I've got, I didn't jump
down Stevie's throat,

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:14.650
this was all in my brain but I'm like

00:45:14.650 --> 00:45:16.220
I've got children and I've got

00:45:19.940 --> 00:45:22.090
all the stuff that I'm doing--
- There is no bingeing.

00:45:22.090 --> 00:45:23.350
- Within my own parameters.
- The only bingeing--

00:45:23.350 --> 00:45:25.320
- I got a bed time!
- The only bingeing

00:45:25.320 --> 00:45:28.010
that has taken place
for me is those two days

00:45:28.010 --> 00:45:30.690
that I was on my back after the vasectomy.

00:45:30.690 --> 00:45:31.760
- Right.
- And you can really only get

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:32.600
a vasectomy once.

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:35.570
I might get my vasectomy reversed
just so I can get it again

00:45:35.570 --> 00:45:37.351
just so I can binge-watch television.

00:45:37.351 --> 00:45:39.330
(Link chuckles)
That's literally the only

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:40.990
binge-watching I've done in 15 years.

00:45:40.990 --> 00:45:42.510
- Stevie's still in her 20s isn't she?

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:45.370
- No she's 31, man.
- (chuckles) Whoops.

00:45:45.370 --> 00:45:47.030
Not in my mind.

00:45:47.030 --> 00:45:48.640
Sorry Stevie.
(Rhett laughs)

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:53.470
I just mean everybody here
is in their 20s in my mind.

00:45:53.470 --> 00:45:55.093
- Okay, thanks for clarifying.

00:45:56.980 --> 00:45:59.593
- Whoops.
(Rhett laughs)

00:46:00.920 --> 00:46:02.620
But you know what I'm saying.
- So what's your point?

00:46:02.620 --> 00:46:05.620
- My point is, what is my point?

00:46:05.620 --> 00:46:10.620
My point is the 20s are the
time for a lot of people

00:46:10.840 --> 00:46:14.040
where it's like, they
just got time to do stuff

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:17.735
but for us, the 20s were oh,

00:46:17.735 --> 00:46:20.820
we've thrust ourselves into marriage.

00:46:20.820 --> 00:46:22.530
- And thrust is probably
not the better word.

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:27.360
- We thrust, we've been
catapulted out of college

00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:32.100
and out from under any
sort of financial support

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:36.790
that our families or whatever offer,

00:46:36.790 --> 00:46:39.130
and now we're trying to make this happen.

00:46:39.130 --> 00:46:41.421
This was like a scrappy time.

00:46:41.421 --> 00:46:43.623
It was nerve-racking.
- Mm-hmm.

00:46:44.697 --> 00:46:48.309
- I was talking about, we
both had our engineering jobs

00:46:48.309 --> 00:46:52.080
and we were hatin' 'em
and I wasn't realize

00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:54.530
I was hating them until you
kept insisting on how much

00:46:54.530 --> 00:46:56.740
I should be hating it,
then I was like yeah man,

00:46:56.740 --> 00:46:59.250
you're right, let's
figure out something else.

00:46:59.250 --> 00:47:02.660
Let's get together one night a
week and come up with comedy.

00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:03.710
- Yeah comedy nights.
- You know,

00:47:03.710 --> 00:47:04.843
come up with comedy.

00:47:06.229 --> 00:47:09.380
Then get up the next morning
and get to the spreadsheets.

00:47:09.380 --> 00:47:14.380
And Christy was teaching,
she had a math degree

00:47:14.840 --> 00:47:18.350
and she taught high school math.

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:22.293
She taught geometry and junk like that.

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:26.150
And she was going crazy,
going nuts just trying

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:28.450
to figure out how to teach high schoolers

00:47:28.450 --> 00:47:33.450
and she was only four
and a half years older

00:47:34.430 --> 00:47:36.401
than some of her students.

00:47:36.401 --> 00:47:37.283
- Mm-hmm.

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:39.810
- She laid down the law the first day,

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:42.113
she was sending people out right and left.

00:47:42.113 --> 00:47:43.363
It was carnage.

00:47:45.650 --> 00:47:46.807
- Paddling them?

00:47:46.807 --> 00:47:47.707
- (sputters) Yeah.

00:47:49.075 --> 00:47:50.410
- She was a paddler.

00:47:50.410 --> 00:47:51.840
- Spank me, Miss Neal.

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:53.310
That doesn't work.

00:47:53.310 --> 00:47:54.760
She didn't fall for that one.

00:47:56.390 --> 00:47:59.500
But so we're both putting
our heads together

00:47:59.500 --> 00:48:03.340
and trying to figure out how we survive.

00:48:03.340 --> 00:48:06.130
- Right but I mean--
- I'm house-sitting

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:08.040
for this couple who was out of the country

00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:10.530
and we're taking care of
a full-grown Weimaraner.

00:48:10.530 --> 00:48:15.200
- Yeah but you look back on
those times with nostalgia.

00:48:15.200 --> 00:48:16.033
- No I don't.

00:48:16.033 --> 00:48:17.610
- I do.
- I do, well--

00:48:17.610 --> 00:48:20.341
- Any time that there's--
- That period was difficult.

00:48:20.341 --> 00:48:22.200
- That scrappy period.
- First year of marriage.

00:48:22.200 --> 00:48:24.520
I wouldn't wish that on my worst enemy.

00:48:24.520 --> 00:48:26.080
- Well let me just tell you the way that--

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:28.153
- And Christy would say the
same thing, you skip that.

00:48:28.153 --> 00:48:30.923
- Well you can't, I
process this in this way.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:34.520
Okay, I'm 41 and...

00:48:39.220 --> 00:48:43.747
Especially related to personal health,

00:48:45.044 --> 00:48:50.044
physical and emotional
health are things that

00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:52.680
it isn't that I haven't
cared about those things

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:57.680
but I have in the past decade or so,

00:48:58.540 --> 00:49:00.863
especially probably the past half decade,

00:49:02.120 --> 00:49:04.730
just getting older has sort
of forced me to be like,

00:49:04.730 --> 00:49:08.320
I've gotta get on top of
this and I'll use my back

00:49:08.320 --> 00:49:12.490
as an example of something
that has sort of applied

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:14.080
to a lot of different areas of my life.

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:15.380
- Talk about your back problems.

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:16.213
- So (chuckles),

00:49:19.130 --> 00:49:21.923
I never did anything about my back

00:49:21.923 --> 00:49:24.320
when I was in high school,
when I was in college.

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:25.897
Never did anything in my 20s.

00:49:27.120 --> 00:49:29.590
Didn't really do anything
about them in my early 30s

00:49:29.590 --> 00:49:32.510
but when it became like okay
this is gonna be a problem,

00:49:32.510 --> 00:49:34.640
if I don't do something about this,

00:49:34.640 --> 00:49:37.250
I'm going to have, I'll be
in a wheelchair someday.

00:49:37.250 --> 00:49:40.860
I will have life-long, I will be disabled.

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.400
I'm not kidding, my back
problems were that bad

00:49:43.400 --> 00:49:44.680
that if I didn't do something about them,

00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:46.030
I was going to be disabled.

00:49:47.020 --> 00:49:52.020
And I started to do the
things that I started to do

00:49:52.700 --> 00:49:57.640
and continue to do and now
it's like my back is healthier

00:49:57.640 --> 00:49:59.430
than it was when I was in high school

00:49:59.430 --> 00:50:00.810
in terms of the way that it feels

00:50:00.810 --> 00:50:02.840
and how often I have pain, right?

00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:05.553
And it's a daily struggle
to continue to do the things

00:50:05.553 --> 00:50:07.070
that I need to do.

00:50:07.070 --> 00:50:09.593
But what I think a lot
of times, I'm like man,

00:50:10.537 --> 00:50:15.537
when I was 22 years old, I
had a 22 year old's spine.

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:18.150
You know what I'm saying?

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:20.900
Like now I've got probably
like a 60 year old spine

00:50:20.900 --> 00:50:23.140
because I didn't do anything all that time

00:50:23.140 --> 00:50:27.365
and so the way I kinda process it is yes,

00:50:27.365 --> 00:50:31.260
those were good times
but I've actually had,

00:50:31.260 --> 00:50:33.530
and again, the back is just
an example of other things

00:50:33.530 --> 00:50:38.530
like again, I started going
to therapy last year so I,

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:41.200
it wasn't like there was some necessarily

00:50:41.200 --> 00:50:43.010
some acute problem, there were some things

00:50:43.010 --> 00:50:44.920
that kinda came up that
led me to finally say,

00:50:44.920 --> 00:50:47.540
okay yeah, I do need to go to therapy.

00:50:47.540 --> 00:50:48.477
But there was a lot of
years where I just--

00:50:48.477 --> 00:50:51.540
- You don't think going
blind was an acute problem?

00:50:51.540 --> 00:50:53.080
- Yeah I'm saying that was
one of the things that--

00:50:53.080 --> 00:50:54.490
- Okay.

00:50:54.490 --> 00:50:56.550
- Wasn't going blind, you
can listen to that podcast,

00:50:56.550 --> 00:50:58.810
but there was an acute problem

00:50:58.810 --> 00:51:00.150
that kinda sent me over the edge.

00:51:00.150 --> 00:51:04.050
All that to say though
is that I feel like now,

00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:08.010
the thing that I can't line
up is caring about yourself

00:51:08.010 --> 00:51:11.250
and taking actual steps
to care for yourself

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:14.563
and set that on top of youth.

00:51:15.650 --> 00:51:18.140
That would be an
incredible thing and maybe,

00:51:18.140 --> 00:51:21.147
maybe that doesn't happen
because when you're young,

00:51:21.147 --> 00:51:25.620
you just don't feel a need
to address these things

00:51:25.620 --> 00:51:26.750
in the same way.

00:51:26.750 --> 00:51:28.820
You're like I've got all my life.

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:31.250
- Youth is wasted on
the young but to answer

00:51:31.250 --> 00:51:35.123
the question, are you saying that the 20s,

00:51:36.200 --> 00:51:38.890
or maybe you're saying
college, either one,

00:51:38.890 --> 00:51:42.974
was the best decade of your life?

00:51:42.974 --> 00:51:46.360
I'm not talking retro-fitted,

00:51:46.360 --> 00:51:48.490
I'm talking about just for what it was.

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:52.660
- I think and maybe this
is just the futuristic,

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:54.653
optimistic part of me,

00:51:57.520 --> 00:52:00.040
I still always have a
sense that I am entering

00:52:00.040 --> 00:52:01.743
into the best years of my life.

00:52:03.357 --> 00:52:05.526
You know what I'm saying, I'm saying--

00:52:05.526 --> 00:52:07.690
- That's weird.
- It didn't get much better.

00:52:07.690 --> 00:52:09.630
- That seems weird to me in a good way.

00:52:09.630 --> 00:52:13.010
- It doesn't get that much
better than what I experienced

00:52:13.010 --> 00:52:15.700
in my 20s and in college
and all those cool memories

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:19.750
or whatever, but there
were things about me,

00:52:19.750 --> 00:52:21.997
the way I thought, the
way I approached life

00:52:21.997 --> 00:52:26.560
in relationships, et cetera, that I'm glad

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:29.250
I don't still think and do the things

00:52:29.250 --> 00:52:30.923
that I did at the time.

00:52:32.050 --> 00:52:35.190
I like the me that I am now more

00:52:35.190 --> 00:52:37.340
than the me that I was then.

00:52:37.340 --> 00:52:40.830
And I would hope that that is
a progression that continues.

00:52:40.830 --> 00:52:45.290
I hope when I'm 50, I look
back at 41 year old Rhett

00:52:45.290 --> 00:52:49.100
and I like the me now more
than I like the me then.

00:52:49.100 --> 00:52:50.410
What else is life about, right?

00:52:50.410 --> 00:52:53.269
Now there's this sort of sinking feeling

00:52:53.269 --> 00:52:58.269
that eventually age will become
an insurmountable problem

00:52:59.130 --> 00:53:01.620
and that no matter what you do,

00:53:01.620 --> 00:53:02.780
you're gonna die of something,

00:53:02.780 --> 00:53:04.760
you're gonna have
chronic pain or whatever,

00:53:04.760 --> 00:53:06.966
maybe there's nothing
they can do about my back

00:53:06.966 --> 00:53:09.220
20 years from now, who knows.

00:53:09.220 --> 00:53:11.532
Not looking forward to that
and answering those questions

00:53:11.532 --> 00:53:15.159
but I'm just saying that
and I told this to Jessie,

00:53:15.159 --> 00:53:17.240
I was like I think my 40s
are gonna be the best years

00:53:17.240 --> 00:53:19.690
of my life and then I think
when I enter into my 50s,

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:22.120
I'm hoping that my perspective will be

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:24.260
I think my 50s are gonna be
the best years of my life.

00:53:24.260 --> 00:53:26.120
- Yeah I--
- That's my attitude.

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:28.330
- I've already ruled out
the 20s and I told you why.

00:53:28.330 --> 00:53:31.930
- Okay.
- So I think for me,

00:53:31.930 --> 00:53:35.343
I think the nows, 'cause I'm 40.

00:53:36.292 --> 00:53:39.100
I don't think it was the 30s.

00:53:39.100 --> 00:53:43.313
I think I agree, I feel like we're on the,

00:53:44.240 --> 00:53:47.450
I'm in year one, you're in year two

00:53:48.330 --> 00:53:50.113
of the best decade.

00:53:51.218 --> 00:53:53.799
I think the 50s won't be as good.

00:53:53.799 --> 00:53:56.437
(Rhett laughs)
Hey, what about grandkids?

00:53:56.437 --> 00:53:57.640
- Why man?

00:53:57.640 --> 00:53:59.690
- I don't think that's gonna help enough.

00:54:01.390 --> 00:54:02.647
I think--

00:54:02.647 --> 00:54:05.380
- Our kids are gonna be like
50 before they have children.

00:54:05.380 --> 00:54:08.120
That's up to them.
- I am the best,

00:54:08.120 --> 00:54:09.900
I feel like I'm the best version of myself

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:14.900
that I've ever been and I want
to be an even better version

00:54:14.949 --> 00:54:19.520
of myself and I think I'm on that journey,

00:54:19.520 --> 00:54:24.520
semi-aggressively and I
think that's a big part of...

00:54:27.170 --> 00:54:31.100
Well, that's a big part of
what makes me satisfied is that

00:54:31.100 --> 00:54:33.080
I feel like I'm growing

00:54:33.080 --> 00:54:36.870
and knowing more about myself.

00:54:36.870 --> 00:54:39.550
Discovering more of
myself and who I can be,

00:54:39.550 --> 00:54:41.600
self-realization I guess you'd call that.

00:54:42.530 --> 00:54:43.650
- And I actually think that there's,

00:54:43.650 --> 00:54:46.960
and I think we grew up with
this, sorry to interrupt

00:54:46.960 --> 00:54:50.543
but to add to what you're saying.

00:54:52.070 --> 00:54:54.913
I think that we kind of had this,

00:54:56.070 --> 00:54:57.160
and I feel like there's a little bit

00:54:57.160 --> 00:55:02.160
of an American ideal here
to be like stay the same.

00:55:03.230 --> 00:55:05.760
He's the guy that he was when he was,

00:55:05.760 --> 00:55:08.247
I'm still the same man that I
was when I was 20 years old.

00:55:08.247 --> 00:55:11.047
I'm still the same, you know
what I'm saying, I'm like--

00:55:14.661 --> 00:55:18.120
- Don't confuse it with a southern accent.

00:55:18.120 --> 00:55:19.050
(both chuckle)

00:55:19.050 --> 00:55:22.450
- I'm saying that I'm from the south.

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:26.120
In many different ways that
mentality was around me.

00:55:26.120 --> 00:55:27.270
- Okay.
- And so that's how

00:55:27.270 --> 00:55:28.990
I'm characterizing it.
- All right that's fine.

00:55:28.990 --> 00:55:30.440
- I'm not making a
judgment about the south,

00:55:30.440 --> 00:55:33.980
I'm making a judgment about who
I was and where I come from.

00:55:33.980 --> 00:55:38.980
And what I'm saying is that I
believe that constant growth

00:55:39.450 --> 00:55:42.903
and constant change is
actually a sign of health.

00:55:44.593 --> 00:55:46.660
Not stagnation.
- Well I mean,

00:55:46.660 --> 00:55:48.117
if it's the right kind
of change of course.

00:55:48.117 --> 00:55:49.043
- Yeah of course.
- Of course.

00:55:49.043 --> 00:55:51.640
- A change in the right direction
but I'm like what is life

00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:55.810
if it isn't change in the right direction?

00:55:55.810 --> 00:55:58.860
If you're not evolving
in a certain direction--

00:55:58.860 --> 00:56:00.770
- You're only dying 'cause you are dying.

00:56:00.770 --> 00:56:03.467
You might as well be growing
and living at the same time.

00:56:03.467 --> 00:56:07.430
- And the idea of settling
into a way of thinking

00:56:07.430 --> 00:56:08.713
and a way of doing,

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:12.012
as soon as I reach my
adulthood and then just being

00:56:12.012 --> 00:56:15.820
that way forever, now
maybe you were enlightened

00:56:15.820 --> 00:56:17.680
at age 21 and you just stayed the same.

00:56:17.680 --> 00:56:19.970
I'm not anywhere near enlightened

00:56:19.970 --> 00:56:22.010
but I'm just trying to
move in that direction

00:56:22.010 --> 00:56:26.160
and so to me that's what
life has become for me

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:29.600
is being like oh, this is what it's about.

00:56:29.600 --> 00:56:31.320
It's about right here,
it's about this moment,

00:56:31.320 --> 00:56:32.670
it's about moving in the right direction.

00:56:32.670 --> 00:56:35.114
- Yeah, let me put it this way.

00:56:35.114 --> 00:56:39.430
There's the phrase live your best life

00:56:39.430 --> 00:56:44.430
and I'm saying that to people constantly.

00:56:44.940 --> 00:56:46.230
Stranger in the malls.

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:49.189
- You and Joel Osteen?
- Live your best life.

00:56:49.189 --> 00:56:50.900
Did he come up with that?
- I think he has a book

00:56:50.900 --> 00:56:52.500
called Live Your Best Life Now.

00:56:52.500 --> 00:56:53.933
- Oh well he added now.

00:56:55.704 --> 00:56:59.660
Well crap, I'm freakin'
deriving a Joel Osteen novel,

00:56:59.660 --> 00:57:01.960
not novel.
- Is it Olsteen or Osteen?

00:57:01.960 --> 00:57:02.863
- Who cares?

00:57:03.760 --> 00:57:04.653
I don't care.

00:57:05.590 --> 00:57:07.090
Osteen.
- Osteen.

00:57:07.090 --> 00:57:08.220
Well you're talking about Olsteen.

00:57:08.220 --> 00:57:10.008
- Ironically--
- You're not talking

00:57:10.008 --> 00:57:10.841
about Olsteen.
- I didn't realize that

00:57:10.841 --> 00:57:14.204
his book was exactly
what I'm saying which is

00:57:14.204 --> 00:57:16.490
life your best life is something

00:57:16.490 --> 00:57:21.490
that you're constantly doing now. (laughs)

00:57:21.620 --> 00:57:24.012
I totally agree with this guy.
- And this is when Joel Osteen

00:57:24.012 --> 00:57:25.700
walks in.

00:57:25.700 --> 00:57:27.923
Fellas, I'm so glad--
- That you finally

00:57:27.923 --> 00:57:30.800
figured it out.
- You finally figured it out.

00:57:30.800 --> 00:57:33.770
Come to my big ol' church. (chuckles)

00:57:33.770 --> 00:57:36.923
We've got 700,000 people every Sunday.

00:57:38.797 --> 00:57:41.253
- It's making a decision to say,

00:57:42.973 --> 00:57:46.260
you tend to focus on the
future and you put it in terms

00:57:46.260 --> 00:57:49.963
of in the future I want
the future to be my best,

00:57:50.960 --> 00:57:54.494
but I want this week or maybe,

00:57:54.494 --> 00:57:56.740
I think it's unfair to say this day.

00:57:56.740 --> 00:57:57.960
Depending on what you're going through,

00:57:57.960 --> 00:57:59.970
it might be this week or this month.

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:01.510
- Just say now like Joel.

00:58:01.510 --> 00:58:06.290
- But I think having a mentality of...

00:58:09.870 --> 00:58:12.170
If I know that I don't
have plans this weekend

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:17.690
and I can either say I'm gonna do nothing.

00:58:17.690 --> 00:58:19.640
I'm gonna let it come to me.

00:58:19.640 --> 00:58:21.820
I feel like for me personally,

00:58:21.820 --> 00:58:24.310
more often than not, that's a mistake

00:58:24.310 --> 00:58:27.960
because it sets me up for
not living my best life--

00:58:27.960 --> 00:58:28.996
- Now.

00:58:28.996 --> 00:58:30.940
(Link chuckles)

00:58:30.940 --> 00:58:33.350
- But instead just kinda waiting for it,

00:58:33.350 --> 00:58:34.670
it's like livin' for the weekend,

00:58:34.670 --> 00:58:36.320
it's like what about Wednesday?

00:58:36.320 --> 00:58:37.860
What's my best Wednesday?

00:58:37.860 --> 00:58:38.693
- Now.

00:58:38.693 --> 00:58:41.590
- Or if I am on the
precipice of the weekend

00:58:41.590 --> 00:58:43.660
or if I'm thinking about it,
it's like what's something

00:58:43.660 --> 00:58:46.070
that I can do that sets up
as we've talked about before

00:58:46.070 --> 00:58:47.440
in the context of the kids,

00:58:47.440 --> 00:58:52.053
what are some things that
we could set up loosely,

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:57.330
not gripped too tightly
like the song goes,

00:58:57.330 --> 00:59:01.360
that will create the
opportunity for an experience

00:59:01.360 --> 00:59:03.760
that could be the best of that.

00:59:03.760 --> 00:59:05.540
The best experience I could have this day

00:59:05.540 --> 00:59:06.950
and that may be sitting on the couch

00:59:06.950 --> 00:59:09.810
and binge-watching whatever Dead to Me is

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:11.520
that I'm never gonna watch, by the way.

00:59:11.520 --> 00:59:12.680
- It's dead to you.

00:59:12.680 --> 00:59:14.060
- But you know what I'm getting at?

00:59:14.060 --> 00:59:15.788
It's like I do feel like--

00:59:15.788 --> 00:59:17.150
- You talkin' about living
your best life now? (chuckles)

00:59:17.150 --> 00:59:20.103
- If you have a mentality of all right,

00:59:21.025 --> 00:59:22.623
I've got this.

00:59:23.516 --> 00:59:28.516
I'm gonna find some enjoyment
or some fill in the blank

00:59:29.070 --> 00:59:30.900
that brings some bestness to it.

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:34.667
- Yeah you talkin' about
gratitude and mindfulness.

00:59:34.667 --> 00:59:37.620
Yeah and that is a challenge for me

00:59:37.620 --> 00:59:40.661
because I am so future-oriented

00:59:40.661 --> 00:59:43.860
and so I was--
- And a lot of people

00:59:43.860 --> 00:59:45.410
are past-oriented, they're like man,

00:59:45.410 --> 00:59:46.600
those were the good ol' days.

00:59:46.600 --> 00:59:47.433
- Yeah.

00:59:47.433 --> 00:59:50.019
- And I look back, I think you looked back

00:59:50.019 --> 00:59:52.150
and I do the same thing and that's like,

00:59:52.150 --> 00:59:55.630
well if I would have
known that I had the back

00:59:55.630 --> 00:59:58.000
of a 20 year old, I would
have done things differently.

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:00.260
And again, that's a revisionist thing--

01:00:00.260 --> 01:00:01.667
- And I can't do anything about it.

01:00:01.667 --> 01:00:03.670
- Can't do anything about it.
- I don't have,

01:00:03.670 --> 01:00:05.380
if I sounded this way, I didn't mean to.

01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:08.360
I don't have regrets about it necessarily,

01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:12.560
I'm just happy that my wife made me go

01:00:12.560 --> 01:00:15.330
to a certain physical therapist

01:00:15.330 --> 01:00:18.380
who finally gave me the right information.

01:00:18.380 --> 01:00:20.610
One thing led to another
and I'm doing things

01:00:20.610 --> 01:00:23.950
that are helping my back
live its best life now.

01:00:23.950 --> 01:00:27.390
- Are we just doing pop
psychology, like vapid,

01:00:27.390 --> 01:00:29.400
empty pop psychology here?

01:00:29.400 --> 01:00:30.473
Or is it just that simple?

01:00:30.473 --> 01:00:32.120
- I honestly don't care if we are.

01:00:32.120 --> 01:00:33.160
If we are, who cares.

01:00:33.160 --> 01:00:35.950
Is it effective?

01:00:35.950 --> 01:00:37.650
Does it make it--
- All we need is love.

01:00:37.650 --> 01:00:39.630
- Does it make a difference?
- Just throw that in there.

01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:44.630
- Because we're pretty
complex creatures, us humans,

01:00:45.110 --> 01:00:48.640
and our brains and, we've
got like we've said before

01:00:48.640 --> 01:00:50.540
many times and somebody
else said it before me,

01:00:50.540 --> 01:00:52.460
we've got Stone Age hardware

01:00:52.460 --> 01:00:55.277
and we're running
modern-day software on it

01:00:55.277 --> 01:00:58.560
and it leads to a lot
of different problems.

01:00:58.560 --> 01:01:02.000
And I think that you can live a life

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:04.687
where you're constantly stressed out

01:01:04.687 --> 01:01:09.340
and there's a bunch of cortisol
in your blood at all times.

01:01:09.340 --> 01:01:10.320
- Yeah.
- Or you can find

01:01:10.320 --> 01:01:13.480
some simple coping
mechanisms that help you

01:01:13.480 --> 01:01:16.570
get out of your own head
and get out of your own way,

01:01:16.570 --> 01:01:18.040
separate yourself from your thoughts

01:01:18.040 --> 01:01:21.910
and realize that you are not your thoughts

01:01:21.910 --> 01:01:26.030
and find some joy in
the moment and also look

01:01:26.030 --> 01:01:27.850
to the future with optimism.

01:01:27.850 --> 01:01:29.760
And I'm not saying that we have done this

01:01:29.760 --> 01:01:32.090
or are experts at it by any means.

01:01:32.090 --> 01:01:33.890
I think just what I'm saying is that

01:01:35.550 --> 01:01:37.830
we spent a lot of time,

01:01:37.830 --> 01:01:41.890
we're so focused on accomplishing
things and succeeding

01:01:41.890 --> 01:01:44.670
at things that I think a good
portion of the last couple

01:01:44.670 --> 01:01:47.504
of decades of our lives have
just been completely consumed

01:01:47.504 --> 01:01:49.557
with trying to succeed.

01:01:49.557 --> 01:01:52.610
We've had no time to stop
and think about things

01:01:52.610 --> 01:01:54.560
like personal health
and growth in many ways

01:01:54.560 --> 01:01:57.210
because we've been so
focused on our career

01:01:57.210 --> 01:01:58.909
and just trying to keep up with things

01:01:58.909 --> 01:02:01.220
and trying to be good
dads and good husbands

01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:03.050
and the things, and the responsibilities

01:02:03.050 --> 01:02:04.430
that we feel like the world has put on us

01:02:04.430 --> 01:02:07.393
but I feel like sort of the one addition

01:02:07.393 --> 01:02:10.210
that has happened to me
in the past few years

01:02:10.210 --> 01:02:14.138
in therapy has been the most
tangible catalyst for this

01:02:14.138 --> 01:02:19.138
is just this sense of working on myself

01:02:19.200 --> 01:02:23.070
and it has made me, the
thing I told Jessie,

01:02:23.070 --> 01:02:26.840
it was like, I feel
just a lot more excited

01:02:26.840 --> 01:02:28.970
about the next chapter of my life.

01:02:28.970 --> 01:02:31.940
If I'm dividing my life into two halves,

01:02:31.940 --> 01:02:34.573
and I'm kinda entering
into the second half,

01:02:36.910 --> 01:02:39.770
I am approaching it with
a lot more excitement

01:02:39.770 --> 01:02:41.980
and not just sense of regret
about what I didn't do

01:02:41.980 --> 01:02:45.320
but more about what I'm gonna get to do

01:02:45.320 --> 01:02:47.800
and of course, it can all fall apart

01:02:47.800 --> 01:02:50.110
and I could face incredible disappointment

01:02:50.110 --> 01:02:52.510
and tragedy and whatever it might be

01:02:52.510 --> 01:02:55.300
but what I'm trying to
do is get to a place

01:02:55.300 --> 01:02:59.050
where the external
circumstances and the outcomes

01:02:59.050 --> 01:03:02.835
don't affect my well-being as much.

01:03:02.835 --> 01:03:05.970
- I think a factor for
me in being in this point

01:03:05.970 --> 01:03:08.780
is the fact that we've
experienced the success we have.

01:03:08.780 --> 01:03:11.310
I feel like in my brain and my heart,

01:03:11.310 --> 01:03:15.370
it's given me the space
to then explore things

01:03:15.370 --> 01:03:17.468
that I didn't give myself permission to do

01:03:17.468 --> 01:03:19.840
and I'm not proud of that.

01:03:19.840 --> 01:03:21.250
I think that's a lesson learned

01:03:21.250 --> 01:03:24.500
that I don't like turning this
into something prescriptive.

01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:26.490
I just like sharing our own experience,

01:03:26.490 --> 01:03:29.663
so I'll keep this on myself
and say to my past self,

01:03:31.421 --> 01:03:34.494
in terms of more self-discovery

01:03:34.494 --> 01:03:39.494
and allowing myself that time for myself,

01:03:40.610 --> 01:03:43.137
even though I was chasing after things

01:03:43.137 --> 01:03:48.137
and the reason why the 20s
were such a perilous time

01:03:49.655 --> 01:03:54.093
was because we were scrappily
trying to make things happen

01:03:54.093 --> 01:03:55.660
and it was...

01:03:58.410 --> 01:04:01.210
There's an art to living
your best life now

01:04:01.210 --> 01:04:03.683
in the midst of those things, right?

01:04:06.410 --> 01:04:10.113
We just happen to be at a point where,

01:04:11.400 --> 01:04:13.071
I mean everything's not going great.

01:04:13.071 --> 01:04:16.810
It's not but there's an art to that,

01:04:16.810 --> 01:04:19.250
whatever life throws at
you, there's still a...

01:04:22.410 --> 01:04:27.150
There's still space for
there to be some bestness

01:04:27.150 --> 01:04:29.963
and you deserve to have some bestness.

01:04:32.980 --> 01:04:33.813
- Yeah.

01:04:33.813 --> 01:04:36.190
- So I'll leave it at
that, but I think it's,

01:04:36.190 --> 01:04:37.927
I'm gonna say not the 20s, the 40s.

01:04:37.927 --> 01:04:40.194
- The 40s.
- The 40s.

01:04:40.194 --> 01:04:41.440
- The 40s.
- The 40s and beyond

01:04:41.440 --> 01:04:44.200
are the best years of our lives.

01:04:44.200 --> 01:04:45.327
And with that--

01:04:45.327 --> 01:04:47.587
♪ Those were the best years of our lives ♪

01:04:47.587 --> 01:04:49.237
- I'm gonna leave you with a rec.

01:04:50.404 --> 01:04:51.412
♪ Rec baby rec baby ♪

01:04:51.412 --> 01:04:52.663
